2.1  What a CPU does: instructions and architectures

TL;DR
  - A CPU runs a stream of machine instructions: load, add, compare, jump,
    store. Which instructions exist, and how they're encoded, is its
    architecture: x86-64, ARM64 (aarch64) or RISC-V.
  - A binary is built for one architecture: an ARM64 program can't run on an
    x86-64 CPU, unless an emulator translates it.
  - Within one architecture, newer CPUs add extensions (AVX2, AVX-512 on x86;
    SVE on ARM). x86-64 groups them into levels v1 to v4.

      uname -m                          # your architecture
      grep -m1 -wo -E 'avx2|avx512f|sve' /proc/cpuinfo

On your machine
  Arch      x86_64, made by Intel
  Level     x86-64-v4: programs built for this level or below run here
  Has       SSE 4.2, AVX2 (256-bit vectors), AVX-512, AES instructions, SHA
            instructions, virtualization (Intel VT-x)
  Flags     145 in all, in /proc/cpuinfo

Pitfalls
  - "Exec format error" usually means a binary for another architecture:
    file ./program says which (23.2).
  - "Illegal instruction" (SIGILL): the program uses an extension this CPU
    lacks, often one built with -march=native on a newer machine.
  - Containers share the host's CPU: an arm64 image on an x86-64 machine only
    runs through QEMU emulation, much slower.
  - Some distros now need a minimum level: RHEL 9 needs x86-64-v2, RHEL 10 needs
    v3. Older CPUs can't install them.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with atfq 2.1 --why

Related
  Commands  uname, lscpu, grep
  Paths     /proc/cpuinfo
  Hub       atfq cpu CPU
  See also  atfq 2.2 Cores, threads and sockets
  See also  atfq 23.2 ELF and "command not found"
